# Pipeline

A CLI job application tracker —  I wanted to actually use the tool I was learning to build.

## Demo
```
$ pipeline add --company "Acme Corp" --role "Backend Engineer" --source referral
Saved application: Acme Corp — Backend Engineer with ID: 1

$ pipeline list
┏━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓
┃ ID ┃ Company    ┃ Role             ┃ Status  ┃ Date Applied ┃
┡━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┩
│ 1  │ Acme Corp  │ Backend Engineer │ applied │ 2026-09-04   │
└────┴────────────┴──────────────────┴─────────┴──────────────┘
```


## Why I built this
Job searching generates a lot of small, easy-to-lose state — who I applied to, when, and what stage I'm at. Spreadsheets work but are tedious to update from the terminal, and every tracker app I found was too rigid to match my own process. I wanted something that took under 10 seconds to update and that I had made.

## Features
- Add an application — company, role, source, link, status defaults to "applied"
- List all applications in a table, most recently updated first
- Update an application's status as it moves through the pipeline
- Archive an application without losing its history
- Filter applications by status and/or company
- **(Stretch) Funnel view** — planned: an ASCII bar chart in the terminal showing conversion between stages

## Installation
```bash
git clone https://github.com/ShamelPanaligan/Job-Application-Tracker
cd pipeline
pip install -e .
```

## Usage
```bash
$ pipeline add --company "Globex" --role "Frontend Engineer" --source "job board"
Saved application: Globex — Frontend Engineer with ID: 2

$ pipeline update 1 --status interviewing
Updated application: Acme Corp — new status: interviewing

$ pipeline filter --status interviewing
